The scrollable overflow of a scroll container puts its padding
after its in-flow content, which ends at the margin boxes of its
block-level children. Ladybird takes the margin past the end of each
box from the flow: the bottom margin of the box, collapsed with the
margins of its contents that collapse through its end. For a box that
margins collapse through, that’s only what its bottom margin adds
to the margins before it.

Blink’s BoxFragmentBuilder::AddChild() does that for its inflow bounds,
with the end margin strut of each child. For a self-collapsing child,
it takes the difference between the sum of the strut with the child’s
bottom margin and without it, and ScrollableOverflowCalculator::Result()
then adds the padding. WebKit’s RenderBlock::computeInFlowOverflow()
adds the content area that the layout of the block ended with, collapsed
margins included, and then scrollablePaddingAreaOverflowRect().
Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::ComputeOverflowAreas() unions the bounds of
the in-flow children of each line, which ReflowBlockFrame() kept with
nsLineBox::SetInFlowChildBounds(), and inflates them by the padding.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s 29
cases come out the same. For an empty last block with a negative bottom
margin, the scroll height in Chrome is larger than in the other two by
that margin, and Ladybird’s is the same as Chrome’s.
